---

Module 1.4 — Completion Standard

Don't mark this module complete merely because you can answer:

> "What is a constructor?"



You should reach:

Explain → Predict → Implement → Trace → Debug → Explain JVM behavior → Handle concurrency → Analyze performance → Design safe initialization → Handle production failures → Answer senior questions

The module's official mastery gate follows essentially this same endpoint: predicting initialization output, understanding <init>/<clinit>, debugging initialization-order problems, understanding concurrency/final fields, inspecting bytecode, discussing performance and designing safe production initialization. 

The most important 1.4 traps to master

> 1. this is not a new object.
2. super is not a separate parent object.
3. Constructors are neither inherited nor overridden.
4. this() and super() cannot both be explicit in one constructor.
5. Static initialization is not the same as class loading.
6. Static initialization happens once per class initialization; instance initialization happens per object.
7. Parent initialization happens before child initialization.
8. Fields follow initialization rules; methods use dispatch rules.
9. Calling overridable methods from constructors can expose partially initialized state.
10. Leaking this during construction can expose an incompletely initialized object.
11. Correct construction is not automatically the same as safe publication.
12. Heavy initialization inside constructors/static blocks is usually a production design smell. 



This makes 1.4 substantially more than a constructor chapter: it is really the bridge between Java OOP → initialization semantics → JVM behavior → Java Memory Model → production object lifecycle.
